\f2\b\fs56 NSScanner Class Cluster\

\fs70 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li200\fc0\cf0 Class Cluster Description\

\fs14 \

\f0\b0\fs28\li1500 An NSScanner object interprets and converts the characters of an NSString into number and string values. You assign the scanner's string on creating it, and the scanner progresses through the characters of that string from beginning to end as you request items. This cluster has a single public class, NSScanner.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The objects you create using this class are referred to as 
\i scanner objects
\i0  (and when no confusion will result, merely as 
\i scanners
\i0 ). Because of the nature of class clusters, scanner objects aren't actual instances of the NSScanner class but one of its private subclasses. Although a scanner object's class is private, its interface is public, as declared by this abstract superclass, NSScanner. (See ªClass Clustersº in the introduction to the Foundation Kit for more information on class clusters and on creating subclasses within a cluster.)\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Scanning Behavior\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Generally, you instantiate a scanner object by invoking the 
\b scannerWithString:
\b0  or
\b  localizedScannerWithString
\b0 : class methods. Either method returns a scanner object initialized with the string you pass to it. The newly created scanner starts at the beginning of its string, progressing through the characters as you request values with 
\b scan...
\b0  methods. You can change the implicit scan location with the 
\b setScanLocation:
\b0  method, to re-scan a portion of the string after an error or to skip ahead a certain number of characters. Scan operations start at the scan location and advance the scanner to just past the last character in the scanned value representation (if any). For example, after scanning an integer from the string ª137 small cases of bananasº, a scanner's location will be 3, indicating the space immediately after the number.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 You can configure a scanner to skip a set of characters with the 
\b setCharactersToBeSkipped:
\b0  method. A scanner ignores characters to be skipped at the beginning of any scan operation. Once it finds a scannable character, however, it includes all characters matching the request. Scanners skip whitespace and newline characters by default. If you continue with the previous example's string and use 
\b scanUpToString:intoString:
\b0  to find the substring before ªofº, the scanner skips the space character before the word ªsmallº but includes the space before ªofº in its result unless you include a space in the search string:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx6740\tx9540\f2\b\fs28\li1900\fc0\cf0 Search String	Result String\

\fs6 \

\pard\tx6740\tx9520\f0\b0\fs28\fi-4840\li6740\fc0\cf0 ªofº (no space before)	ªsmall cases º (includes the space following)\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ª ofº (space before)	ªsmall casesº (stops before the space)\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 You can also configure a scanner to consider or ignore case using the 
\b setCaseSensitive:
\b0  method. By default a scanner ignores case. Note that case is always considered with regard to characters to be skipped. To skip all English vowels, for example, you must set the characters to be skipped to those in the string ªAEIOUaeiouº.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 A scanner bases some of its scanning behavior on a locale, which specifies a language and conventions for value representations. NSScanner uses only the locale's definition for the decimal separator (given by the key named NSDecimalSeparator). You can create a scanner with the user's locale by using 
\b localizedScannerWithString:
\b0 , or set the locale explicitly using
\b  setLocale:
\b0 . If you use a method that doesn't specify a locale, the scanner assumes the default locale values. See ªLocalesº in the ªOther Featuresº section of the Foundation Kit documentation for more information on locales.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 For an example of using a scanner, suppose you have a string containing lines such as:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2320\tx3740\tx4140\fs28\li2320\fc0\cf0 Product: Acme Potato Peeler; Cost: 0.98\
Product: Chef Pierre Pasta Fork; Cost: 0.75\
Product: Chef Pierre Colander; Cost: 1.27\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 This method scans such a string to extract the product information for each line:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 - (BOOL)scanProductString:(NSString *)string\
\{\
    NSCharacterSet *semicolonSet;\
    NSScanner *theScanner;\
    NSString *PRODUCT = @"Product:";\
    NSString *COST = @"Cost:";\
    NSString *productName;\
    float productCost;\
\
    semicolonSet = [NSCharacterSet\
        characterSetWithCharactersInString:@";"];\
    theScanner = [NSScanner scannerWithString:string];\
\
    while ([theScanner isAtEnd] == NO) \{\
\
        if ([theScanner scanString:PRODUCT intoString:NULL] &&\
            [theScanner scanUpToCharactersFromSet:semicolonSet\
                        intoString:&productName] &&\
            [theScanner scanString:@";" intoString:NULL] &&\
            [theScanner scanString:COST intoString:NULL] &&\
            [theScanner scanFloat:&productCost]) \{\
\
            /* Do something with productName and productCost. */\
        \}\
        else return NO;\
    \}\
\
    return YES;\
\}\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 This method uses alternating scan operations to skip the expected substrings ªProduct:º and ªCost:º, as well as the semicolon, and to read the values for the product name and cost (read as a 
\b float
\b0  for simplicity's sake). It returns NO if an error occurs on any scan operation, and YES if it successfully scans and processes all lines. Note that because a scanner skips whitespace and newlines by default, the loop does no special processing for them.\

\fs14 \

